SEC. 90001. BORDER INFRASTRUCTURE AND WALL SYSTEM.

Appropriates $46,550,000,000 to the Commissioner of U.S. Customs and Border Protection (CBP) for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for border infrastructure and wall system expenses. Funds may be used for construction, installation, or improvement of primary, waterborne, and secondary barriers; access roads; barrier attributes like cameras, lights, and sensors; and ground preparation work near the border to support CBP operations.

SEC. 90002. U.S. CUSTOMS AND BORDER PROTECTION PERSONNEL, FLEET VEHICLES, AND FACILITIES.

Appropriates $4,100,000,000 to CBP for hiring and training additional Border Patrol agents, Office of Field Operations officers, Air and Marine agents, rehired annuitants, and field support personnel, available until September 30, 2029. Also appropriates $2,052,630,000 for recruitment, performance, and retention bonuses for eligible agents and officers; $855,000,000 for repair and acquisition of patrol vehicles; and $5,000,000,000 for facility and checkpoint expenses. Funds are restricted from being used to recruit, hire, or train processing coordinators after October 31, 2028.

SEC. 90003. DETENTION CAPACITY.

Appropriates $45,000,000,000 to U.S. Immigration and Customs Enforcement (ICE) for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for single adult alien detention capacity and family residential center capacity. Aliens may be detained at family residential centers pending removal decisions and until removal, with detention standards for single adults set at the Secretary of Homeland Security's discretion. Defines "family residential center" as a DHS facility detaining family units of aliens, excluding unaccompanied alien children.

SEC. 90004. BORDER SECURITY, TECHNOLOGY, AND SCREENING.

Appropriates $6,168,000,000 to CBP for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for border security technology and screening. Funds cover nonintrusive inspection equipment (including AI and machine learning), Air and Marine operations platform upgrades, border surveillance technologies, biometric entry-exit systems, screening of persons entering or exiting the U.S., initial screenings of unaccompanied alien children, and efforts to combat drug trafficking (including fentanyl). Funds are prohibited from being used for surveillance towers along the southwest and northern borders that have not been tested and accepted by CBP to deliver autonomous capabilities.

SEC. 90005. STATE AND LOCAL ASSISTANCE.

Appropriates $500,000,000 for State and local capabilities to detect and monitor unmanned aircraft systems threats, $625,000,000 for security costs related to the 2026 FIFA World Cup, $1,000,000,000 for security costs related to the 2028 Olympics, and $450,000,000 for the Operation Stonegarden Grant Program, all administered by FEMA under the State Homeland Security Grant Program. Also establishes a State Border Security Reinforcement Fund with $10,000,000,000 appropriated for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2034, for grants to States and local governments for border wall construction, ground preparation, detection and interdiction of illicit substances and unlawfully present aliens, and relocation of unlawfully present aliens from small population centers. The Secretary of Homeland Security must begin accepting applications within 90 days of enactment, and FEMA may use up to 1% of these funds for administration.

SEC. 90006. PRESIDENTIAL RESIDENCE PROTECTION.

Appropriates $300,000,000 to FEMA for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, to reimburse State and local agencies for extraordinary law enforcement personnel costs directly associated with protecting a President's residence designated under the Presidential Protection Assistance Act of 1976. Funds are available only for costs incurred on or after July 1, 2024, that exceed typical law enforcement costs, are directly attributable to Secret Service-requested protection, and are certified by the agency. FEMA may use up to 3% of these funds for administration.

SEC. 90007. DEPARTMENT OF HOMELAND SECURITY APPROPRIATIONS FOR BORDER SUPPORT.

Appropriates $10,000,000,000 to the Secretary of Homeland Security for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for reimbursement of costs incurred in activities supporting DHS's border safeguarding mission.

SEC. 90101. FEHB IMPROVEMENTS.

Requires the Director of the Office of Personnel Management (OPM) to issue regulations and implement a process within one year to verify the veracity of qualifying life events and that individuals added to Federal Employees Health Benefits (FEHB) plans are qualifying family members. Requires the Director to include an assessment of ineligible enrollees in any fraud risk assessment of the program. Mandates a comprehensive 3-year audit of covered family members, reviewing marriage and birth certificates, and requires development of a disenrollment process within 180 days. Amends 5 U.S.C. 8909 to set aside $66,000,000 in fiscal year 2026, available until the end of fiscal year 2035, for OPM to carry out these verification and audit functions.

SEC. 90102. PANDEMIC RESPONSE ACCOUNTABILITY COMMITTEE.

Appropriates $88,000,000 for fiscal year 2026, available until expended, to the Pandemic Response Accountability Committee for oversight of the Coronavirus response and funds provided in this Act or other pandemic-related Acts. Amends the CARES Act to add this reconciliation Act to the list of covered funds and extends the Committee's authority from 2025 to 2034.

SEC. 90103. APPROPRIATION FOR THE OFFICE OF MANAGEMENT AND BUDGET.

Appropriates $100,000,000 to the Office of Management and Budget for fiscal year 2025, available until September 30, 2029, for finding budget and accounting efficiencies in the executive branch. [end of text]


